t, rather than showing only that an unrestricted dictionary definition would be unhelpful for distinguishing competing accounts. Locked score: The move earns 75 because its conditional logic, illustration, and clarity are strong, but reduced responsiveness and charity materially limit its relevance to the newly specified account.\n      \n    \n  \n        \n    \n      \n        Red herring\n      \n      \n        Red herring\n        Logical fallacy\n        Diverting attention from the issue under dispute to a related but less relevant point, leaving the original challenge insufficiently answered.\n        Kastrup attacks illusionism as broadly covering nearly every scientific correction after Blackmore narrows its target.\n        Open the reference page for more.\n      \n    \n  \n      \n    \n  \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:00:35\n    \n  \n        Major direct reply\n        86\n      \n      \n      Blackmore locates illusion in metacognitive stories of a self owning experience, citing meditation in which experience continues without an owner, time, or spatial framing.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        86/100 · Major direct reply\n        Strongest feature: Blackmore turns a broad method into a specific target: reflective stories of an owner can disappear while experience continues, supporting a distinction between underlying occurrence and constructed metacognitive interpretation. Principal limitation: Her meditation report is personal and interpretively loaded, so it cannot by itself establish that ownership, time, and space are always false rather than temporarily absent or differently represented during unusual attention states. Live burden: She must connect this phenomenology to publicly testable evidence and clarify which metacognitive claims are erroneous without denying the practical organization supplied by self-models. Locked score: The move earns 86 because it is highly responsive, precise, charitable, and central to her burden, though its evidential base and underlying ontology remain deliberately limited.\n      \n    \n  \n        No named fallacy\n      \n    \n  \n            \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:05:35\n    \n  \n        Major direct reply\n        80\n      \n      \n      Kastrup agrees that selfhood and dualist categories can mislead, then recasts needles, wine, and other apparent physical causes as representations of mental interactions.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        80/100 · Major direct reply\n        Strongest feature: Kastrup acknowledges genuine convergence with Blackmore and applies his ontology to concrete causal examples, explaining apparent physical-to-mental influence as mental activity crossing a dissociative boundary. Principal limitation: The reinterpretation is coherent within idealism but largely presupposes that all material objects represent mentality, so it does not independently establish the ontology or explain detailed causal regularities. Live burden: He must show that the account yields constraints, predictions, or comparative explanations beyond translating physical descriptions into mental terms, while retaining Blackmore's narrower diagnosis of reification. Locked score: The move earns 80 because it is clear, relevant, and substantially responsive, with good calibration in acknowledging agreement, yet its evidence and explanatory leverage remain incomplete.\n      \n    \n  \n        No named fallacy\n      \n    \n  \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:04:20\n    \n  \n        Major direct reply\n        87\n      \n      \n      Blackmore specifies that illusionism targets reifying consciousness as a separable entity produced by brains, not the broader claim that every physical appearance is illusory.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        87/100 · Major direct reply\n        Strongest feature: Blackmore answers the overbreadth objection with her clearest boundary: the target is metalevel talk that turns consciousness into a separate thing and then asks how brains produce it. Principal limitation: She still offers limited evidence that this conceptual diagnosis explains the relevant data, and her language shifts among everyday concepts, scientific theories, and introspective constructions without fully separating those levels of analysis. Live burden: She must identify which claims are dispensable reifications, show how removing them changes research practice, and avoid treating every production model as dualistic by definition or linguistic habit. Locked score: The move earns 87 because it directly resolves the live semantic challenge with precision, relevance, and unusual charity, though the method's positive empirical payoff remains less developed than its diagnostic force.\n      \n    \n  \n        No named fallacy\n      \n    \n  \n            \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:10:55\n    \n  \n        Supporting reply\n        76\n      \n      \n      Kastrup treats laws as primitive properties of nature and the personal ego as a useful metacognitive narrative layered upon an organism's body schema.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        76/100 · Supporting reply\n        Strongest feature: Kastrup accepts an ultimate explanatory stopping point shared by metaphysical rivals and offers a functional reason for self-representation, distinguishing basic experience, body schema, and reflective metacognition. Principal limitation: The fork-to-mouth example initially overstates the ego's necessity, as Blackmore's animal objection shows, and saying nature behaves according to its properties explains little about why idealism should posit those properties. Live burden: He must clarify which coordination functions require metacognitive selfhood rather than body schema alone and how the layered model follows from analytic idealism specifically, not merely cognitive organization. Locked score: The move earns 76 because it is coherent, responsive, and reasonably calibrated after clarification, but its evidence and distinct explanatory contribution remain modest.\n      \n    \n  \n        No named fallacy\n      \n    \n  \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:09:37\n    \n  \n        Supporting reply\n        89\n      \n      \n      Blackmore concedes that physics also cannot explain why fundamental constants have their values, so ultimate regularity cannot fairly be demanded from idealism alone.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        89/100 · Supporting reply\n        Strongest feature: Blackmore openly limits her strongest recurring objection, recognizing that explanations in any framework may terminate at brute constants or laws and refusing to impose an asymmetric burden on idealism. Principal limitation: The concession concerns ultimate values, not intermediate questions about whether Kastrup's model explains structured correlations, perceptual representation, or the emergence of biological boundaries, so its scope must remain carefully narrow. Live burden: She should preserve those local explanatory challenges while granting that no rival supplies an endless chain of deeper reasons for every regularity. Locked score: The move earns 89 because its logic, responsiveness, clarity, and especially calibration are excellent, with strong comparative relevance and only the ordinary evidential limits inherent in a philosophical concession.\n      \n    \n  \n        No named fallacy\n      \n    \n  \n            \n      \n    \n  \n    \n      \n        \n          \n    \n      1:18:05–1:28:59\n    \n  \n          Revisability, telepathy, and materialism\n        \n        \n          \n    \n      Analytic idealism\n      62\n    \n  \n          \n    \n      Illusionist critique\n      84\n    \n  \n        \n      \n      \n        \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:19:40\n    \n  \n        Supporting reply\n        69\n      \n      \n      Kastrup argues that imperfect dissociation could allow tiny, emotionally conditioned information leakage while leaving laboratory controls and ordinary scientific results largely intact.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        69/100 · Supporting reply\n        Strongest feature: Kastrup anticipates an apparent conflict with scientific practice by limiting any proposed porosity to weak, context-sensitive effects, preserving the everyday privacy of minds and most experimental controls. Principal limitation: He offers no independent evidence that emotional circumstances weaken dissociation, and explaining laboratory non-detection through stronger laboratory boundaries risks insulating the claim from the controlled tests needed to establish it. Live burden: He must state conditions that make leakage predictably detectable and distinguish his account from coincidence, selective recall, or other ordinary explanations. Locked score: The move earns 69 because it is intelligible and responsive as a compatibility argument, but its empirical warrant, calibration, and falsifiability remain substantially weaker than its metaphysical possibility claim.\n      \n    \n  \n        \n    \n      \n        Special pleading\n      \n      \n        Special pleading\n        Logical fallacy\n        Applying an exception or different standard to one side of the argument without giving a principled reason for the difference.\n        Kastrup says telepathic leakage occurs in emotional life but disappears under controlled laboratory conditions.\n        Open the reference page for more.\n      \n    \n  \n      \n    \n  \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:18:05\n    \n  \n        Supporting constructive\n        85\n      \n      \n      Blackmore would revise her understanding of mind if adequately designed, sufficiently powered, statistically sound experiments produced convincing evidence of telepathy.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        85/100 · Supporting constructive\n        Strongest feature: Blackmore states a concrete revision standard and explains its consequence: reliable telepathy would undermine informational independence assumed in psychological controls and alter interpretation across placebo and related research. Principal limitation: Her initial suggestion that most science would be wrong is broader than the specific methodological disruption she later describes, and she does not define an effect size, replication threshold, or acceptable protocol. Live burden: She must apply the same openness to credible contemporary evidence while distinguishing serious anomalies from results produced by weak design, multiplicity, or leakage. Locked score: The move earns 85 because it is clear, falsifiable, responsive, and methodologically grounded, with only bounded overstatement and incomplete operational detail preventing an even stronger result.\n      \n    \n  \n        No named fallacy\n      \n    \n  \n            \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:21:46\n    \n  \n        Supporting reply\n        61\n      \n      \n      Kastrup recounts his girlfriend's detailed dream about her grandmother and the confirming callback as a personally compelling but uncontrolled case of possible information leakage.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        61/100 · Supporting reply\n        Strongest feature: Kastrup supplies a concrete chronology with multiple matching details: his girlfriend described her grandmother bandaged beside two daughters, then a callback revealed a recent stroke and reassuring medical status. Principal limitation: The report is retrospective, secondhand in crucial parts, and uncontrolled, leaving coincidence, memory reconstruction, unnoticed cues, and selective emphasis unevaluated; it therefore establishes no independently verified paranormal event. Live burden: He must document timing and prior statements, compare alternative explanations, and show that such cases occur above an appropriate base rate before treating them as evidence for porous dissociation. Locked score: The move earns 61 because its narrative is specific and clear, but evidential warrant, responsiveness to controlled-evidence demands, and calibration are weak.\n      \n    \n  \n        \n    \n      \n        Base-rate neglect\n      \n      \n        Base-rate neglect\n        Cognitive bias\n        Underweighting general background rates or prior probabilities while focusing too heavily on a vivid specific case or local detail.\n        Kastrup treats one vivid dream match as compelling without comparing ordinary dream and coincidence frequencies.\n        Open the reference page for more.\n      \n    \n  \n    \n      \n        Subjective validation\n      \n      \n        Subjective validation\n        Cognitive bias\n        Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.\n        Kastrup says a personally witnessed dream coincidence was so specific he could not dismiss it.\n        Open the reference page for more.\n      \n    \n  \n      \n    \n  \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:21:10\n    \n  \n        Supporting reply\n        86\n      \n      \n      Blackmore welcomes testable telepathy but argues that parapsychology has not produced convincing evidence and viable theory sufficient for controlled comparison of mechanisms.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        86/100 · Supporting reply\n        Strongest feature: Blackmore distinguishes excitement about a possible phenomenon from belief that it has been established, and connects credible evidence to the next scientific step of testing competing mechanisms. Principal limitation: Her broad historical conclusion is asserted without reviewing particular studies in this exchange, and her later admission of limited familiarity with research after about 2000 restricts how confidently it applies to current literature. Live burden: She must assess representative contemporary evidence under her announced design and statistical standards, rather than relying solely on the field's past failure to mature into cumulative science. Locked score: The move earns 86 because it directly answers Kastrup's compatibility claim with disciplined evidential standards and strong calibration, despite compressed documentation and dated coverage.\n      \n    \n  \n        No named fallacy\n      \n    \n  \n            \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:25:09\n    \n  \n        Load-bearing constructive\n        57\n      \n      \n      Kastrup calls materialism explanatorily deficient, internally contradictory, and empirically inadequate, declining an ordinary revision condition while contrasting a concrete hypothetical multiverse test.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        57/100 · Load-bearing constructive\n        Strongest feature: Kastrup consolidates three distinct objections and usefully contrasts his refusal with a specific kind of anomalous energy result that could increase his confidence in a multiverse. Principal limitation: The answer largely evades the requested revision condition, caricatures materialism through the Flying Spaghetti Monster comparison, and presents contested interpretations of quantum experiments and intellectual history with excessive certainty. Live burden: He must formulate the strongest coherent materialist alternative, identify evidence that would discriminate it from idealism, and defend rather than assume his empirical and historical claims. Locked score: The move earns 57 because it contains a recognizable comparative argument, but weak warrant, poor responsiveness, and severely disproportionate calibration overwhelm its clarity and relevance.\n      \n    \n  \n        \n    \n      \n        Confirmation bias\n      \n      \n        Confirmation bias\n        Cognitive bias\n        Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.\n        Kastrup says changing his view would require becoming a child and unlearning forty-eight years.\n        Open the reference page for more.\n      \n    \n  \n    \n      \n        Red herring\n      \n      \n        Red herring\n        Logical fallacy\n        Diverting attention from the issue under dispute to a related but less relevant point, leaving the original challenge insufficiently answered.\n        Asked for a revision condition, Kastrup shifts to the Flying Spaghetti Monster, church politics, and Enlightenment history.\n        Open the reference page for more.\n      \n    \n  \n      \n    \n  \n              \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      1:23:35\n    \n  \n        Supporting reply\n        80\n      \n      \n      Blackmore declines an instant case judgment, explaining that fair parapsychology review is laborious and that her deep literature familiarity ended around the turn of the century.\n      \n        \n    \n      ◉\n      \n        80/100 · Supporting reply\n        Strongest feature: Blackmore resists giving a glib explanation of Kastrup's anecdote, separates the host's researcher prompts from her own commitments, and candidly dates the limits of her expertise. Principal limitation: She does not answer the particular case or substantiate her reference to problematic practices, so the reply offers disciplined scope control rather than a substantive rebuttal of the alleged coincidence and its evidential force. Live burden: She must examine the documentation and relevant current literature before drawing a case-specific or field-wide conclusion, while no concession follows merely from declining hurried analysis of an unfamiliar retrospective report. Locked score: The move earns 80 because its charity, caution, and clarity are strong, but its responsiveness and evidence are necessarily bounded by the explicit refusal to adjudicate immediately.\n      \n    \n  \n        No named fallacy\n      \n    \n  \n            \n      \n    \n  \n      \n    \n      \n        Final read\n        Overall commentary\n      \n      \n        \n    \n      \n        Analytic idealism · Bernardo Kastrup\n        75\n      \n      Landed\n      \n        Kastrup offers a coherent one-category ontology that directly addresses the hard problem by refusing any fundamental transition from nonexperience to experience.His dashboard account separates external reality from perception while explaining selective representation through biological usefulness and manageable informational demands.The dissociation proposal combines a clear inferential-isolation concept with clinical evidence, giving private minds a more concrete mechanism than simple assertion.\n      \n      Whiffed\n      \n        Kastrup repeatedly moves from compatible observations to specifically idealist conclusions without showing why rival ontologies cannot explain the same patterns. Confirmation biasHis closing materialism answer supplies no usable revision condition and treats a contested dream coincidence as suggestive despite lacking controlled verification. Base-rate neglect Confirmation bias Subjective validation\n      \n    \n  \n        \n    \n      \n        Illusionist critique · Susan Blackmore\n        85\n      \n      Landed\n      \n        Blackmore consistently identifies the missing comparative bridge: calling nature mental must explain regularity, representation, neural correlation, and individual boundaries better.She narrows illusionism to a critical method about ownership and reification, avoiding both denial of experience and an unnecessary complete rival metaphysics.Her strongest replies combine precise questions with fair concessions, especially granting brute regularities and declining to adjudicate unfamiliar parapsychology evidence hastily.\n      \n      Whiffed\n      \n        Her early materialist comparisons overstate what physical theory explains, a weakness she later acknowledges when conceding that ultimate constants remain brute. Her meditation-based account of ownerless experience remains personally reported and underconnected to public evidence, limiting its reach beyond methodological suggestion. \n      \n    \n  \n      \n    \n  \n      \n    \n      \n        \n          AI contribution\n          AI Contribution\n        \n        An AI-generated steelman of where each case could go next.\n      \n      \n        \n          \n            Explore the AI-generated arguments\n            Strengthened final arguments and new reinforcements for both sides\n          \n          \n        \n        \n          \n            This section is an AI-generated contribution for Slugfester—not a transcript summary, a quotation, or a claim that any speaker made these arguments in this form. The AI independently extends and strengthens both positions against the clearest objections raised in the exchange. It does not affect the debate scores.\n          \n          \n            \n    \n      \n        Analytic idealism\n        Bernardo Kastrup\n      \n\n      \n        Strengthened final argument\n        Analytic idealism best explains consciousness by treating experience as fundamental, physical appearances as representations, and private minds as informationally isolated regions within one field of subjectivity.\n        \n          \n                \n                  Premise 1\n                  Experience is immediately given in ordinary awareness, whereas a wholly nonexperiential substrate is theoretical and leaves the emergence of qualitative awareness conceptually unexplained.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Premise 2\n                  Treating external nature and perception as different kinds of mental state permits causal interaction without crossing an ontological divide between matter and lived experience.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Premise 3\n                  Perception need not reproduce reality itself, because evolved organisms benefit from compressed dashboards that preserve action-relevant structure while hiding overwhelming underlying environmental detail.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Premise 4\n                  Clinical and ordinary dissociations show that one experiential system can contain informationally isolated processes, making divided subjectivity at least conceptually coherent and empirically motivated.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Premise 5\n                  Neural correlations can be interpreted as the external appearance of dissociated mental activity, preserving established scientific findings without identifying brains as the literal producers of experience.\n                \n              \n        \n        \n          Conclusion\n          A single experiential ontology therefore unifies consciousness, perception, and neural correlation more economically than materialism, provided dissociative boundaries can be independently characterized and tested.\n        \n      \n\n      \n        New reinforcing arguments\n        \n          \n                \n                  Comparative Prediction Ledger\n                  Idealism gains explanatory force only when its reinterpretation of evidence constrains expectations differently from rivals. Kastrup could specify a ledger of cases in which identity between mental activity and physical appearance predicts correlations, dissociative failures, or perceptual distortions that a production model would not independently expect. A critic may object that mature neuroscience could absorb every listed pattern without changing ontology. That objection makes comparative risk essential: the ledger must include outcomes that would count against idealism, not merely phenomena it can redescribe afterward. Even one well-defined divergent prediction would convert ontological economy from an aesthetic advantage into an empirically discriminating research claim.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Boundary Hierarchy Test\n                  Dissociation becomes more credible as a cosmic mechanism if boundaries form a nested hierarchy governed by one observable principle. Kastrup could predict that stronger inferential isolation tracks more stable biological integration, while partial boundary failures produce graded, reproducible information exchange rather than all-or-nothing privacy. The obvious objection is that this pattern may simply restate familiar neural connectivity without supporting universal consciousness. To answer, the account must identify a measure derived from idealism that applies across clinical divisions, ordinary organisms, and any alleged anomalous leakage. Failure of that measure would weaken the metaphysics; success would connect local evidence to the disputed world-scale extrapolation.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Economy With Exclusions\n                  Removing a nonmental ontic category is valuable only if the resulting theory also excludes incoherent or unsupported possibilities. Kastrup could strengthen his economy argument by deriving limits on which experiential structures can appear as stable physical worlds, rather than allowing any regularity as a primitive disposition of mind. Blackmore can fairly object that every metaphysics eventually reaches brute facts, but that concession does not erase intermediate explanatory duties. If idealism identifies structural constraints linking qualitative relations, perceptual dashboards, and lawful public measurements, it offers more than relabeling. Without such exclusions, simplicity remains genuine but shallow; with them, economy could yield a disciplined explanatory program.\n                \n              \n        \n      \n    \n  \n            \n    \n      \n        Illusionist critique\n        Susan Blackmore\n      \n\n      \n        Strengthened final argument\n        Analytic idealism remains underjustified because its central observations fit multiple ontologies, while dissociation, representation, and mental causation are described without sufficiently discriminating mechanisms or evidence.\n        \n          \n                \n                  Premise 1\n                  Illusionism can target mistaken metacognitive stories about ownership, temporal unity, and reification without denying experience or committing its critic to a materialist ontology.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Premise 2\n                  Perceptual dashboards explain why appearances differ from reality, but that functional account remains compatible with materialist, panpsychist, and idealist interpretations of the underlying world.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Premise 3\n                  Clinical dissociation establishes informational division inside particular nervous systems, not the existence or partition of a universal subject extending beyond all biological organization.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Premise 4\n                  Calling neural activity the outward appearance of experience preserves observed correlation, yet does not derive specific pairings or outperform rival causal interpretations of neurological evidence.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Premise 5\n                  Personal coincidence reports may motivate serious inquiry, but rational revision should depend on documented, controlled, replicated evidence assessed against ordinary alternative explanations and relevant base rates.\n                \n              \n        \n        \n          Conclusion\n          The critical verdict should therefore remain provisional but unfavorable: idealism is coherent and economical, yet it has not earned adequacy through distinctive explanations, boundary criteria, or controlled support.\n        \n      \n\n      \n        New reinforcing arguments\n        \n          \n                \n                  Asymmetric Detail Challenge\n                  A successful metaphysics need not explain why every ultimate law holds, but it should explain why some intermediate structures follow from its commitments. Blackmore could separate brute constants from derivable architecture and ask idealism to predict features of perceptual compression, neural correlation, or biological individuation. Kastrup may answer that physical theories also begin from unexplained structures. That reply is fair only at the same explanatory level: shared ignorance about constants does not excuse missing links between universal mentality and particular organisms. The consequence is a sharper burden that respects Blackmore’s concession while preserving her central criticism of explanatory relabeling.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Ownership Intervention Program\n                  Blackmore’s claim about constructed ownership can move beyond meditation reports through interventions that alter metacognition while preserving task-relevant experience. Studies could compare confidence, temporal self-location, agency reports, and perceptual discrimination before and after targeted attention training or neural disruption. A result showing altered ownership alongside stable discrimination would support her distinction, though it would not prove that ownership is always illusory or settle ontology. Null results, or changes confined to verbal report, would narrow the thesis. This program converts a philosophical method into public evidence while respecting the limited claim that some reflective interpretations, not experience itself, are mistaken.\n                \n              \n                \n                  Anecdote-to-Evidence Protocol\n                  Reports like Kastrup’s girlfriend’s dream deserve neither dismissal nor immediate metaphysical weight. Blackmore could propose prospective timestamping, preregistered matching criteria, blinded judges, comparison pools, and replication across emotionally salient relationships. Critics may worry that laboratory structure suppresses the very conditions under which weak effects occur. The protocol can therefore include naturalistic prospective collection while preserving advance records and explicit base rates. If matches remain exceptional under those safeguards, competing mechanisms become worth testing; if they do not, memory and coincidence retain priority. This approach makes openness and skepticism operational rather than treating either a vivid narrative or field history as decisive.\n                \n              \n        \n      \n    \n  \n          \n        \n      \n    \n  \n      \n    Keep exploring\n    Related debates\n    Follow the question further, or hear a familiar interlocutor in a different exchange. Suggestions reflect topics and participants, not an endorsement of a position.\n    \n      Explore the same topicShares this debate’s primary topic; selected for overlap in its question and section headings.\n      \n    \n      \n        \n    \n      225\n    \n  \n        Published or updated Sep 1, 2026\n      \n      Does Analytic Idealism Explain Consciousness Better? Bernardo Kastrup vs. Tom Jump2023\n      Analytic idealism and materialist explanation\n      \n        \n          \n    \n\n    \n  \n    \n\n    \n  \n        \n        \n          83\n          /\n          80\n        \n      \n    \n  \n    \n      A different matchupAnother debate featuring Bernardo Kastrup, with a different participant lineup.\n      \n    \n      \n        \